<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Three Leagues</h1><p class="st-meta">1471 CE – 1799 CE (approx.) · capital Chur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Alpine republic of the Grisons, a federation of three leagues of communes that ruled the Valtellina and allied with the Swiss for three centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/league-of-gods-house">League of God's House</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grey-league">Grey League</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/league-of-the-ten-jurisdictions">League of the Ten Jurisdictions</a> formed alliances with one another during the 15th century, completed by 1471. Acting together as the Free State of the Three Leagues, they became associates of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy</a> in 1497 and 1498 and fought beside the Swiss in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-war">Swabian War</a> of 1499.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a> lay with the communes, which voted on major decisions, an early form of direct democracy. A common diet met in rotation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chur">Chur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilanz">Ilanz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/davos">Davos</a>. In 1512 the leagues conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valtellina">Valtellina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bormio">Bormio</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiavenna">Chiavenna</a>, which they ruled as subject lands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graubunden">Grisons</a> troubles of 1618 to 1639, a civil war entangled with Spanish and French interests, tore the state apart.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1797 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valtellina">Valtellina</a> broke away and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cisalpine-republic">Cisalpine Republic</a>. In 1799 French forces attached the leagues to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>, and in 1803 they became the Swiss canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graubunden">Graubünden</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/three-leagues.jpg" alt="Three Leagues" width="320" height="229"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was Elvezio at it.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tre_leghe_grigioni.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1471 CE – 1799 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chur</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Reformed Protestantism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Romansh, German, Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Switzerland, Italy</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Free State of the Three Leagues, Grisons, Drei Bünde</dd></dl></aside></div>
